---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import GuideTable from '../../components/GuideTable.astro';
import { bySlug, destMonthUrl, deg, MONTHS } from '../../lib/data';

const ALL = MONTHS.map((_, i) => i);
const rows = [
  { slug: 'cairns', label: 'Cairns & the Great Barrier Reef (tropical north)' },
  { slug: 'whitsundays', label: 'Whitsundays (tropical north)' },
  { slug: 'uluru', label: 'Uluru (Red Centre)' },
  { slug: 'gold-coast', label: 'Gold Coast (subtropical east)' },
  { slug: 'sydney', label: 'Sydney (temperate south-east)' },
  { slug: 'perth', label: 'Perth (Mediterranean west)' },
  { slug: 'melbourne', label: 'Melbourne (temperate south)' },
  { slug: 'great-ocean-road', label: 'Great Ocean Road (temperate south)' },
  { slug: 'tasmania', label: 'Tasmania (cool south)' },
];
const cairns = bySlug.get('cairns')!;
const melbourne = bySlug.get('melbourne')!;
---

<Guide slug="best-time-to-visit-australia-north-vs-south" photo="whitsundays">
  <p>
    Australia is so big that its seasons run in opposite directions. The <strong>tropical north</strong> — Cairns, the Great
    Barrier Reef, the Whitsundays and Darwin — is best in the dry winter, from <strong>May to October</strong>, and hot, humid
    and stormy in the summer wet season (December–March). The <strong>south</strong> — Sydney, Melbourne, Tasmania and Perth
    — is best in summer and the shoulder seasons, from <strong>October to April</strong>, and cool in winter. The Red Centre is
    best in between, in autumn, winter and spring.
  </p>

  <h2>Weather rating by month</h2>
  <GuideTable caption="Weather rating across Australia by month" months={ALL} rows={rows} />
  <p class="small muted">Ratings combine average temperature and rainfall from Bureau of Meteorology normals.</p>

  <h2>North vs south</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <b>Tropical north (May–October):</b> dry, sunny and warm — in July, Cairns averages <a href={destMonthUrl(cairns, 6)}>{deg(cairns.months[6].high)}</a> by day. The wet season (December–March) brings monsoon rain, cyclone risk and marine stingers in the sea, but also green landscapes, waterfalls and lower prices. See the <a href="/guides/great-barrier-reef/">Great Barrier Reef guide</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Temperate south (October–April):</b> warm summer days for beaches, coastal drives and festivals; Melbourne averages <a href={destMonthUrl(melbourne, 0)}>{deg(melbourne.months[0].high)}</a> in January, but only <a href={destMonthUrl(melbourne, 6)}>{deg(melbourne.months[6].high)}</a> in July. March–May brings autumn colours in Tasmania and the Victorian high country.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Red Centre (April–September):</b> Uluru and the outback are pleasant in the cooler months — sunny days, cold nights — and extremely hot from November to February.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The west:</b> Perth has a Mediterranean climate with hot, dry summers; August–October is wildflower season in Western Australia. See <a href="/guides/wildflower-seasons/">wildflower seasons</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>One trip, both halves</h2>
  <p>
    To see both the reef and the south in one trip, aim for <b>April–May</b> or <b>September–November</b>: the north is drying
    out or still dry, and the south is mild and pleasant. December–January are the summer school holidays, when beaches, flights
    and hotels are busiest and most expensive.
  </p>
  <p>
    See also: <a href="/best-time-to-visit-australia/">the best time to visit Australia</a> month by month and{' '}
    <a href="/guides/whale-watching/">whale watching</a> (humpbacks migrate along the east coast from June to November).
  </p>
</Guide>
